//!
//! # The problem this solves
//!
//! Under managed auth each application completes its own provider ceremony on
//! its own host, so each one needs its own Google and GitHub redirect URI
//! registered by hand. Eight properties meant thirty-two console entries, and
//! every new property meant two more — plus a realm configuration-epoch
//! advance to change them, which is the operation that has broken every
//! upgrade attempt on this fleet.
//!
//! # The shape
//!
//! One property is the **broker** (`login.<base>`). On upstream Core it
//! performs the Google or GitHub ceremony itself through `packages/auth`.
//! Only that host is registered with Google and GitHub: **two callbacks per environment,
//! forever**.
//!
//! Every other owned property is a **client**. It runs without managed auth,
//! bounces anonymous visitors to the broker, and receives back a short-lived,
//! audience-bound, single-use [`assertion`] naming the verified subject. It
//! then mints its own host-only session.
//!
//! ```text
//!   docs.<base>/protected          (anonymous)
//!     -> 303 login.<base>/sso/start?client_id=docs&return_to=/protected
//!          [the broker verifies the person: Google or GitHub, packages/auth]
//!     -> 303 docs.<base>/_sso/callback?a=<assertion>&return_to=/protected
//!          [verify MAC, audience, expiry; burn nonce; mint session]
//!     -> 303 docs.<base>/protected  (signed in)
//! ```
//!
//! Adding a property is a row in the broker's [`registry`] and a redeploy. No
//! console change, and no epoch advance.
//!
//! # What is given up, stated plainly
//!
//! Client properties leave managed auth, so **the gateway no longer refuses
//! anonymous traffic at the edge for them** — the application does. Managed
//! auth fails closed before application code runs; this does not. The trade is
//! reasonable for first-party properties under one operator and is not
//! reasonable for tenant applications.
//!
//! The assertion key is symmetric and shared, so any client could mint for any
//! other. See [`assertion`] for why that is bounded here and what the upgrade
//! path is.

